  "slug": "arg-imbalance-871a",
  "summary": "Sequence-to-function models now test archaic introgressed variants directly, exposing a modality-dependent gap in prediction accuracy.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is recovery of admixture history and functional consequences from patterns in genomic variation. In a preprint, AlphaGenome scores 144,139 introgressed SNPs in Papuan genomes, with chromatin predictions matching assays but expression predictions not matching. A second preprint, Restrictome-EVOLVE, applies matched-control haplotype analysis to 30 antiviral restriction-factor loci across 660 genomes and reports reduced population differentiation at these loci. Published work extends the methodological toolkit: a SNP panel of about 1M sites selected for minimal technology-specific bias enables coanalysis of capture and shotgun ancient DNA, and a bivariate marker-effect framework separates large-covariance outlier loci from polygenic background correlation in genetic correlations. A pangenome of 120 Mexican tetra fish addresses recurrent cave-specific deletions, adjacent in scope rather than focused on ancestry. What is new is the sequence-to-function test of introgressed variants, which reveals a modality-dependent gap, alongside matched-control haplotype analysis of restriction-factor loci.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-09-07"
}
